WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Cardiac output sits where the heart's rising line crosses the veins' falling line.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.740
Now a drug relaxes blood vessels, and resistance falls.

3
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.340
Does right atrial pressure rise, or fall?

4
00:00:16.817 --> 00:00:18.477
Take one change at a time.

5
00:00:19.677 --> 00:00:24.037
If only one line moves, the new crossing must still sit on the line that stayed.

6
00:00:25.237 --> 00:00:27.757
So the crossing slides along the unmoved line.

7
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.540
The heart's line slopes up.

8
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.580
Slide along it, and output and atrial pressure rise or fall together.

9
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.340
The veins' line slopes down.

10
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.400
Slide along it, and one rises as the other falls.

11
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.910
So here is the rule.

12
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:50.010
Find the line that shifted, then slide along the one left standing.

13
00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:55.490
Move the veins' line, and output and atrial pressure go the same way.

14
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.230
Move the heart's line, and they go opposite ways.

15
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.690
Back to the drug. Resistance fell, but which resistance?

16
00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.810
Total peripheral resistance is measured from arterial pressure.

17
00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.450
With an illustrative arterial pressure, it comes out at 18.

18
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.890
Resistance to venous return is 1.

19
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.730
So say which vessels dilated.

20
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.370
Suppose the veins dilate.

21
00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:28.050
They hold more blood at the same pressure, so filling pressure falls.

22
00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.750
Only the veins' line moves, sliding left.

23
00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:36.790
Slide down the heart's line: output and atrial pressure both fall.

24
00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:43.733
A different case. Halve the resistance to venous return, and change nothing else.

25
00:01:44.993 --> 00:01:48.673
The veins' line tilts steeper, pivoting at its zero-flow point.

26
00:01:49.953 --> 00:01:52.413
Which way do output and atrial pressure go?

27
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.320
Only the veins' line moved, so slide along the heart's line.

28
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:10.640
Both rise. Atrial pressure goes from 2 to about 3.7 mmHg.

29
00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.700
Output goes from 5 to about 6.7 L per min.

30
00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:18.863
Arteriolar dilation does more.

31
00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:23.843
It lowers resistance to venous return, tilting the veins' line.

32
00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:28.423
It also lowers afterload, which can lift the heart's line.

33
00:02:29.663 --> 00:02:32.103
Two lines move, so take each shift in turn.

34
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:34.803
Both shifts raise output.

35
00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:40.063
For atrial pressure they pull opposite ways, and the larger shift wins.

36
00:02:41.463 --> 00:02:42.903
In this model the tilt wins.

37
00:02:44.603 --> 00:02:48.983
Output reaches about 7.3, and atrial pressure about 3.3.

38
00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:53.043
Those sizes were chosen for clarity, not measured.

39
00:02:54.117 --> 00:02:56.377
So did atrial pressure rise or fall?

40
00:02:57.557 --> 00:02:59.317
It depends which vessels relaxed.

41
00:03:00.617 --> 00:03:04.977
Spot the shifted line, and the unmoved one shows where the circulation settles.
